"""Traced from the watercolour the bouquet is modelled on.

Lengths are in stage em (the stage is 20em wide); polygons are clip-paths, in percent of the
shape's own box. Every shape is painted as a stack of flat washes: its whole outline in its
body colour first, then the areas darker than that, then the lighter ones, palest last.

LEAVES   x, y: where the leaf springs from; dir: the way its tip points (degrees clockwise
         from up); w, h: its box, standing upright on that point
FLOWERS  x, y: middle of the bloom; s: half the side of its box; r: its radius;
         qx, qy: the point it opens from (and its stem runs to); body: its silhouette;
         deep: washes that lie under the petals, drawn in from their edge (seen when two part);
         petals, outermost first: box, ux/uy: the way the petal lies from that point, dist: how far
RIBBON   a box and its washes
STEMS    the cut stems below the ribbon, a strip for each: its box, the point it hangs
         from under the ribbon (px, py), and its washes
"""

